# Nesting HTML Elements

HTML elements can contain other HTML elements. This is called _nesting_. For example, in the following code:

```{html autorun}
<p>This is a <strong>bolded</strong> word</p>
```

The `<strong>` element is nested inside the `<p>` element. This means that the text "bolded" will be displayed in bold, while the rest of the text will be displayed normally.

![A diagram showing one element nested inside another](images/nested-element-illustration.png)

## Terminology

In the code sample above (`<p>This is a <strong>bolded</strong> word.</p>`), there is standard terminology:

- The `<p>` element is the **parent** of the `<strong>` element.
- The `<strong>` element is the **child** of the `<p>` element.

Whenever nesting elements, it is important to make sure elements are _fully nested_, meaning that every child element is fully contained within its parent element. For example, the following code is **not** fully nested:

```html
<p>This would <em>not be</p> fully nested.</em>
```

In the example above, the `<em>` element (which creates emphasized text that is italicized) is not fully contained within the `<p>` element, which makes it invalid HTML (though browsers will try to fix it).

![Diagram of improperly nested tags: an opening p tag, an opening em tag, a closing p tag, then a closing em tag, with the p and em ranges drawn as crossing arcs instead of one sitting inside the other](images/not-fully-nested.png)

We could fix it by fully moving the `<em>` element inside its parent, such as:

```{html autorun}
<p>This would <em>be</em> fully nested.</p>
```

## Exercises

1. Answer the following question about nesting terminology:

   ```{mcq}
   Consider the code `<div><p>Hello</p></div>`. Which of the following statements is true?
   correct: Nice work. The `p` element is nested inside the `div` element, so the `div` is the parent and the `p` is the child.
   wrong: Not quite. Look at which element is nested inside the other: the `p` element is inside the `div` element.
   [x] The `div` element is the parent of the `p` element.
     correct: Right. The `p` element is nested inside the `div` element, so the `div` is its parent.
     wrong: This is the correct statement. The `p` element is nested inside the `div` element, so the `div` is its parent.
   [ ] The `p` element is the parent of the `div` element.
     wrong: The `p` element is nested inside the `div` element, so the `p` is the child, not the parent.
   [ ] The `div` element is the child of the `p` element.
     wrong: The `div` element contains the `p` element, so the `div` is the parent, not the child.
   ```

2. Create a `p` element with the text "This is very important", where the words "very important" are bolded (using a `strong` element nested inside the `p` element).

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <p>This is <strong>very important</strong></p>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('p').should('exist').run('Could not find a p element', 'Created a p element')
   assert('p strong').should('exist').run('The p element does not contain a strong element', 'Created a p element with a strong element')
   assert('p').should('have.text', 'This is very important').run('The p element does not have the text "This is very important"', 'The p element has the text "This is very important"')
   assert('p strong').should('have.text', 'very important').run('The strong element should contain the text "very important"', 'The words "very important" are bolded')
   ```

3. Create an `h1` element with the text "Welcome to my page", where the word "my" is emphasized (using an `em` element nested inside the `h1` element).

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <h1>Welcome to <em>my</em> page</h1>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('h1').should('exist').run('Could not find an h1 element', 'Created an h1 element')
   assert('h1 em').should('exist').run('The h1 element does not contain an em element', 'Created an h1 element with an em element')
   assert('h1').should('have.text', 'Welcome to my page').run('The h1 element does not have the text "Welcome to my page"', 'The h1 element has the text "Welcome to my page"')
   assert('h1 em').should('have.text', 'my').run('The em element should contain the text "my"', 'The word "my" is emphasized')
   ```

4. Create an unordered list (`ul`) element that contains two list item (`li`) elements: the first with the text "Milk" and the second with the text "Apples". Make sure both `li` elements are fully nested within the `ul` element.

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <ul>
       <li>Milk</li>
       <li>Apples</li>
   </ul>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('ul').should('exist').run('Could not find a ul element', 'Created a ul element')
   assert('ul li').should('exist').run('The ul element does not contain any li elements', 'Created a ul element with li elements')
   assert('ul li:nth-child(1)').should('have.text', 'Milk').run('The first li element does not have the text "Milk"', 'The first list item has the text "Milk"')
   assert('ul li:nth-child(2)').should('have.text', 'Apples').run('The second li element does not have the text "Apples"', 'The second list item has the text "Apples"')
   ```

